# Markdown: prettier has NO authority here, so make that explicit (#3947).
#
# `format:check` is `prettier --check 'src/**/*.ts' 'tests/**/*.ts'
# 'scripts/**/*.ts'` — it checks ZERO markdown. So running `prettier --write`
# on a .md file is all risk and no benefit: nothing verifies the result, and
# prettier rewrites the WHOLE file rather than the part you edited. Measured
# on origin/main, 2026-08-01:
#   docs/ci-policy.md   5 +/6 -  — and one of those is CONTENT DAMAGE, not
#                                  restyling: a backtick span adjacent to
#                                  **bold** makes prettier mis-parse the
#                                  emphasis run and DELETE the spaces between
#                                  words (`src/**`stays both-lane` —`target:).
#                                  Third occurrence of this mangling, second
#                                  file (cf. 7327b3ac).
#   README.md          17 +/12 -  (tables realigned, *em* -> _em_ in prose)
#   ROADMAP.md         18 +/16 -  (same)
# Ignoring them turns `prettier --write <doc>` into a loud no-op instead of a
# silent rewrite, which closes the class without asking anyone to remember
# which files are gated.
#
# CLAUDE.md is DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED from this ignore. It is the one markdown
# file whose prettier-cleanliness is actually asserted by a gate
# (`sync:conformance:check` + tests/issue-3947.test.ts): the sync script now
# emits the exact blank-line shape prettier wants, and `prettier --write
# CLAUDE.md` producing no diff is the live evidence that the two agree.
# Ignoring it would make that check pass because prettier cannot see the file
# — a detector that cannot fail is not a detector.
**/*.md
!/CLAUDE.md
